diagnosis_text:
There is no network fault. Direct diagnostic tests — pings in both directions
between PC1 (10.1.0.100 on aswa2) and PC2 (10.1.0.101 on aswa3) — returned
0% packet loss with sub-millisecond round-trip times. A one-hop traceroute
(TTL=64) confirms the two hosts are reachable at Layer 2 without any routing
boundary. The default gateway (10.1.0.1) is also fully reachable from PC1.
Both access switches, aswa2 and aswa3, have all ports in the UP state with
healthy Linux bridge instances (br0 on each), and aswa2's MAC address table
correctly shows PC1's MAC learned on its local port and PC2's MAC learned via
the uplink — exactly the expected forwarding state for a multi-switch L2
domain.

The user's ticket contains two factual misconceptions. First, PC1 and PC2 are
NOT on the same physical access switch: PC1 connects via the link_a2_pc
segment to aswa2, while PC2 connects via CD_CLIENT_A3 to aswa3. These are
two separate access switches in the Office-A stack, both uplinked to the
distribution layer (dswa1/dswa2). Second, neither switch has failed, nor has
any port been disabled — all interfaces are operationally UP and forwarding.
The shared VLAN spanning aswa2→dswa1/dswa2→aswa3 is intact and fully
functional. The reported outage does not exist in the current lab state; no
corrective action is required.
=== END ===
